<p>An index earns its place by removing work the application repeatedly needs. For an order-history page, that might mean finding one tenant’s recent orders in the right order and stopping after ten, instead of inspecting every order and sorting the survivors. The same index also has to accept new orders and changes to old ones.</p>
<p>Before starting, finish <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a>, including <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/#change-one-explanation-into-a-measured-improvement">Change one explanation into a measured improvement</a>. Its comparison method is the one used here. This article designs the candidate and explains the work it should remove; no sibling article is a prerequisite.</p>
<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="start-with-the-queries-the-workload-must-serve">Start with the queries the workload must serve</h2>
<p>The shop serves several businesses from one orders table. A <strong>tenant</strong> is one of those businesses. Mina’s history page belongs to tenant 42 and asks for its ten newest orders since a date:</p>
<Sql code={history} label="The history query used throughout this article" />
<p>The date and tenant restrict the answer; the two ordering columns decide which ten come first. <code>order_id</code> breaks ties between equal timestamps. The query returns <code>total</code> but does not search or sort by it. Those are different jobs for an index column.</p>
<p>Record the other work before choosing a key. The page can optionally show pending orders only. Opening the page also fetches its orders’ lines, by <code>order_id</code>. New orders arrive, statuses change and totals can be corrected. A less frequent daily report groups orders by their UTC date. These requests compete for a small useful set of indexes; the first fast history query cannot decide that set.</p>
<p>For each statement, retain its predicates, join keys, ordering, grouping, returned columns, frequency and common and slow parameter classes. Record existing constraints and indexes, row count, tenant sizes, status distribution and change rate. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">application query capture</a> for the actual SQL and preparation path, rather than inferring them from a screen or an ORM method name.</p>
<p>We use PostgreSQL 18.6 for captured execution. The disposable fixture has 30,000 orders, 100 equal-sized tenants and 300 orders per tenant, spread through the table by arrival time. Five per cent are pending. The history query has 170 qualifying orders and returns ten. This deliberately regular fixture exposes mechanisms; it does not represent the shop’s eventual skew or concurrent traffic.</p>
<p>The following reporting legend applies to every capture below:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Estimated rows</strong> predict output from a plan node; <strong>actual rows</strong> report output during execution. Neither automatically counts every entry inspected. PostgreSQL reports actual rows and rows removed as per-loop averages; alongside <code>loops=10</code>, <code>actual rows=3</code> means about 30 rows emitted in total. Rounding can affect that multiplication.</li>
<li><code>Index Cond</code> means an index condition was used. It does not prove every listed column narrowed one contiguous interval. <code>Filter</code> and <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code> identify later rejection. The captures do not provide a general counter of all B-tree leaf entries tested; invented entry counts belong only to the labelled models.</li>
<li><code>Heap Fetches</code> reports heap fetches by an index-only scan. An ordinary index scan does not print that field, so its absence does not mean zero table visits.</li>
<li><code>shared hit</code> counts buffer accesses satisfied in PostgreSQL’s cache; <code>shared read</code> counts blocks loaded into it, possibly from the operating system’s cache. They are neither distinct-page counts nor physical-device I/O. Parent counters include child work: the comparison tables use the top node once.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF)</code> runs the statement and captures this evidence. Use disposable data for the worksheet. For deeper reading of estimates and plan choices, <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">Diagnosing a query from its execution plan</a> is optional.</p>

<h2 id="follow-a-b-tree-from-key-to-row">Follow a B-tree from key to row</h2>
<p>A <strong>B-tree</strong> keeps keys sorted in pages. Its root and any intermediate pages direct a search towards a range; its bottom, or <strong>leaf</strong>, pages contain the entries for actual rows. An entry holds a key and a <strong>row locator</strong>: the information needed to find the base row. Finding the first entry, scanning further entries and visiting base rows are three separate pieces of work.</p>
<p>Figure 1 draws a deliberately small two-level tree over twelve orders. Choose tenant 42 and follow each locator. Then broaden the request to two tenants and compare the table pages reached.</p>
<IndexFigure scene="traversal" number={1} />
<p>The narrow lookup reaches four table pages. The broader one reaches all six, after doing index work as well. A scan can then be a cheaper route. These are model counts with tiny invented page capacities; no cache or device timings are simulated. Real trees have many entries per page, so their depth grows slowly. A range descends to its starting point and continues along neighbouring leaf pages until its bound or the caller’s limit ends the request.</p>
<p>In PostgreSQL an ordinary entry’s locator identifies a heap page and item. In InnoDB a secondary entry carries the clustered key, which starts a search in the table’s own tree. Either way, neighbours in the secondary index can lead to scattered base rows. The exact locator and physical placement affect what a visit costs; <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">How stored rows become physical work</a> owns the page, cache and version mechanisms behind that cost.</p>
<p><strong>Selectivity</strong> is the fraction of rows matching a condition. A selective lookup can avoid most table work. A broad one may reach most pages, particularly when matches are scattered. Cache state and locality change the price of those visits, and an ordered first page may stop much earlier than a full result. There is no fixed matching percentage at which an index becomes worthwhile.</p>

<h2 id="put-composite-keys-in-the-order-the-query-can-use">Put composite keys in the order the query can use</h2>
<p>A <strong>composite key</strong> sorts by its first column, then by the next within equal first values. For the history query, fixing tenant 42 leaves one date-ordered run. The date restriction bounds that run, and the descending order lets the scan stop after ten.</p>
<Sql code={candidate} label="A key derived from equality, date order and its tie-breaker" />
<p>Figure 2 uses the same twelve orders. The request asks for up to two orders since 5 January. Compare the normal history with a key that puts status between tenant and date. Then supply a pending-only condition and watch that extra equality change the useful range.</p>
<IndexFigure scene="keys" number={2} />
<p>Putting status before date creates one date run per status. That is useful when status is fixed; when it is absent, the first paid orders are not necessarily the newest orders overall. Putting date first interleaves tenants. It can supply date order, but may pass many other tenants’ entries before filling one tenant’s first page. “Put the most selective column first” does not explain either result.</p>
<p>In the baseline B-tree model, equality on leading keys and a range on the next establish the interval. A test on a later key may reject entries without making that interval smaller. If status comes after the date keys, for example, it can reject non-pending entries in the index while the scan still traverses their positions. A condition absent from the index needs the base row before it can be tested.</p>
<p>This is a starting model, not an absolute leftmost-prefix rule. PostgreSQL 18 can use <strong>skip scan</strong>, making repeated searches across omitted leading values when it estimates that worthwhile. A missing leading condition can also leave a broad index scan useful for ordering or coverage. Check the selected path and observed work; a later column appearing under <code>Index Cond</code> does not establish a short scan. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html">multicolumn-index rules</a> describe those opportunities.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed history access with different keys"><table><caption>Captured history query · ten identical returned rows · every node loops once</caption><thead><tr><th>Only candidate beside the primary key</th><th>Observed route and work</th><th>Top shared hits / reads</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>No history candidate</td><td>Sequential scan: 170 emitted, 29,830 rejected; sort</td><td>613 / 0</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>(tenant_id)</code></td><td>Bitmap route: 300 heap rows tested, 130 rejected; sort</td><td>300 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>(placed_at DESC, tenant_id, order_id DESC)</code></td><td>Index scan; ten emitted; no sort</td><td>13 / 5</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>(tenant_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code></td><td>Index scan; ten emitted; no sort</td><td>10 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>(tenant_id, status, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code></td><td>Bitmap route: 170 heap rows emitted; sort</td><td>172 / 4</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The tenant/date candidate removes both broad heap filtering and sorting for this request. The date-first capture also avoids a sort, but does more buffer work. We have not measured its total leaf entries tested. The small difference between 18 and 12 accesses is not a measured latency ratio; the two captures also contain different mixes of hits and reads.</p>

<h3 id="join-lookups-and-several-query-shapes-compete-for-key-order">Join lookups and several query shapes compete for key order</h3>
<p>The related-row request supplies an <code>order_id</code> for each history result and needs that order’s lines. A candidate therefore begins with <code>order_id</code> on <code>order_lines</code>, rather than copying the history key onto that table. Its existing primary-key index on <code>(order_id, line_no)</code> already supplies that prefix; another plain <code>order_id</code> index needs a separate justification.</p>
<p>In the native fixture, 90,000 lines give every order three lines. The selected nested loop reads ten history orders and probes the existing line index ten times. The inner bitmap heap node reports <code>actual rows=3 loops=10</code>: three rows per probe, 30 overall. This demonstrates an existing access role without making nested loops obligatory for a broad report. <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">Execution-plan diagnosis</a> follows join-method choices when repeated work becomes the problem.</p>
<p>The pending-only history changes the trade-off. With tenant/date alone, this fixture finds 170 candidate rows and rejects 162 by status. With tenant/status/date it finds the eight pending rows directly. PostgreSQL chooses a bitmap route and a small sort in both captures; fewer rows are not a promise of an ordered index scan.</p>
<p>Separate tenant and status indexes are another possible set. PostgreSQL can combine their row-location bitmaps, but that involves reading both structures and loses their key order. It is not automatically equivalent to one composite route that finds a bounded, ordered first page. Nor must one composite index support every combination of optional filters. Choose the set from the frequent combinations and their costs, rather than adding one index per screen control.</p>

<h3 id="ordering-and-grouping-can-change-the-useful-route">Ordering and grouping can change the useful route</h3>
<p>An ordered route earns most when the caller stops early. Without <code>LIMIT</code>, many base-row visits can cost more than a table scan and one sort. Keep <code>ORDER BY</code> in the SQL: an index offers a cheap implementation of the order, not a result-order guarantee.</p>
<p>A B-tree can be traversed backwards, reversing all its key directions. After fixing the tenant, an ascending date/identifier pair can therefore supply both descending. A request mixing ascending date with descending identifier needs that combination or an additional sorting step. Nullable keys also have an order: PostgreSQL defaults to <code>NULLS LAST</code> ascending and <code>NULLS FIRST</code> descending. A request for descending dates with nulls last needs an appropriate route. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-ordering.html">ordering rules</a> make these choices explicit.</p>
<p>Ordering can survive one stage and be insufficient at the next. Our related-line capture preserves history order through its nested loop, then uses an <code>Incremental Sort</code> to complete the requested line-number ordering within each order. Likewise, an index on a grouping key can feed an ordered grouping operation, but filters, joins and a selected bitmap or hash route can change that opportunity. Grouping still has to consume all relevant input rows. <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/">Joining and summarising data</a> owns aggregate meaning and <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#order-and-page-a-result-deliberately">Working with SQL</a> owns pagination correctness.</p>

<h2 id="decide-which-row-visits-are-worth-avoiding">Decide which row visits are worth avoiding</h2>
<p>The history key lacks <code>total</code>. Fetching the ten totals requires ten base rows even though the search itself is well shaped. A <strong>covering index</strong> holds every value a particular query needs. PostgreSQL’s <code>INCLUDE (total)</code> stores total as a leaf payload; it does not make total part of the search or ordering key.</p>
<p>Holding the values makes an <strong>index-only scan</strong> possible, but PostgreSQL must still establish which row versions the query may see. A compact visibility map records whether every row on a heap page is visible to all relevant readers. A set all-visible bit avoids the heap check; a clear bit requires it. Figure 3 separates that decision from whether total is present.</p>
<IndexFigure scene="coverage" number={3} />
<p>Try “one page changed”. A change clears that page’s all-visible status, so even a different, unchanged order sharing the page may require a heap fetch. <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">The storage article</a> explains the underlying versions and cleanup; here the practical test is the observed fetch count.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Coverage and actual heap fetches"><table><caption>The same covering history index and ten returned rows · PostgreSQL 18.6</caption><thead><tr><th>Fixture state</th><th>Observed Heap Fetches</th><th>Top shared hits / reads</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Freshly loaded, before vacuum</td><td>10</td><td>10 / 2</td></tr><tr><td>After vacuum</td><td>0</td><td>3 / 0</td></tr><tr><td>After changing another order on one relevant page</td><td>1</td><td>4 / 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<pre class="a25-plan"><code>{covered}</code></pre>
<p>This shortened after-vacuum plan names an index-only scan and reports zero heap fetches. The first capture also names an index-only scan, with ten heap fetches. Trust the field, not the node name alone. Automatic vacuum was disabled only on this disposable fixture to isolate the transition; manual vacuum established the all-visible state.</p>
<p>Coverage trades row visits for stored copies and maintenance. Wider payloads fit fewer leaf entries into each page, though alignment and allocation mean every added small value need not grow the file immediately. Here both freshly built history indexes, with and without the integer total, occupied 1,228,800 bytes. That observation does not make extra columns free: total changes now affect an indexed value. Frequently changing pages can also remove much of the expected heap-avoidance benefit. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-index-only-scans.html">covering-index documentation</a> recommends weighing those conditions.</p>

<h2 id="check-that-the-query-is-eligible-to-use-the-index">Check that the query is eligible to use the index</h2>
<p>A useful order is only a hypothetical route until the actual query can use it. Check the compared expressions, operand types, operators and text collation. An implicit conversion can occur on the parameter or on the indexed column; those are different access questions. A B-tree operator class defines which comparisons its stored order supports, including supported cross-type comparisons.</p>
<p>Our integer predicate <code>tenant_id = 42</code> uses the history route. Changing it to <code>tenant_id::text = '42'</code> produced a sequential scan and sort, with 613 shared hits, despite the history index. Converting the stored integer to text changes the indexed expression. Bind an integer when integer identity is intended; do not rewrite a query without checking that its results still mean the same thing.</p>
<p>A text index’s collation defines its comparison and ordering rules. An explicit different collation can therefore require another route, even when the column name matches. Check the index definition and actual comparison rather than treating every function, conversion or text operator as equally indexable. PostgreSQL documents <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-opclass.html">operator families</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-collations.html">index collations</a> separately because they answer separate parts of that check.</p>

<h3 id="index-a-subset-or-an-expression-when-that-removes-real-work">Index a subset or an expression when that removes real work</h3>
<p>The pending queue is a small active subset. A <strong>partial index</strong> stores entries only for rows satisfying its predicate:</p>
<Sql code={partial} label="Keep the pending subset in tenant/date order" />
<p>This fixture’s pending index occupied 81,920 bytes, compared with 1,228,800 for the full history key. Paid inserts do not enter it; changing a pending order to paid creates removal and cleanup obligations, and changing paid to pending creates an entry. NULL status is not pending: equality to pending evaluates to unknown for that row and excludes it.</p>
<p>The planner must prove that the query asks only for rows in the subset. Figure 4 compares a known pending value with a generic status parameter whose value is unavailable during planning.</p>
<IndexFigure scene="subset" number={4} />
<p>We prepared the same status/tenant query and deliberately forced each plan mode for comparison, then reset the session setting. The custom plan, built with pending known, used the partial index and ten shared hits. The generic plan, valid for any status value, scanned the table and used 613 hits. Both returned the same eight pending orders. This is a captured eligibility difference, not advice to force custom plans. A prepared query that writes the pending predicate literally can still parameterise tenant or date without hiding that predicate. Test the application’s real form; <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">plan diagnosis</a> owns caching decisions.</p>
<p>An <strong>expression index</strong> instead stores a transformed key. The daily report needs dates in UTC. Casting a timestamp with time zone directly to date depends on the session time zone, and PostgreSQL rejected that index definition as non-immutable. Fixing UTC gives one stable expression:</p>
<Sql code={expression} label="Index the UTC day used by the daily report" />
<p>The matching count used a bitmap index route with 33 total buffer accesses and returned 1,440. A direct <code>placed_at::date</code> predicate scanned 613 cached buffers and also returned 1,440 in this UTC session. The equal count here does not make the expressions equivalent under another time zone. A timestamp range over the original time key is another candidate when its day boundaries preserve the intended meaning. Expression evaluation is paid during inserts and relevant updates. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-expressional.html">Expression indexes</a> and the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html">definition rules</a> specify these restrictions.</p>

<h3 id="change-index-family-only-for-an-access-need-b-trees-do-not-meet">Change index family only for an access need B-trees do not meet</h3>
<p>Suppose order metadata now carries several labels and the shop needs containment, such as <code is:raw>metadata @&gt; '{"gift":true}'::jsonb</code>. Sorting whole documents in a B-tree does not provide that membership route. PostgreSQL’s GIN index can index keys and values inside <code>jsonb</code>. The default <code>jsonb_ops</code> supports containment and key-existence operators; <code>jsonb_path_ops</code> supports containment and selected JSON-path operators but not key existence. Neither supplies the history query’s date order.</p>
<p>That access need justifies evaluating a different family. Inspect any recheck work, because a candidate returned by an index need not itself establish the final predicate. Use the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/datatype-json.html#JSON-INDEXING">documented JSON indexing operators</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/json/">Working with JSON in PostgreSQL</a> for the feature-specific design. The native worksheet did not measure this extension.</p>

<h2 id="count-the-obligations-created-by-each-candidate">Count the obligations created by each candidate</h2>
<p>Every insert adds entries to applicable indexes. A changed indexed key or payload creates more maintenance; a deletion leaves cleanup work after its logical effect. Wider entries consume more space and cache, full pages can split, and many writers targeting the same key range can compete internally. A unique index also checks for conflicting keys. Count that work at the workload’s actual change rate, including the log, cleanup and build budget.</p>
<p>Coverage makes this trade concrete. Figure 5 changes total while leaving the search keys alone. The model assumes room for a same-page version and compares a key-only index with one including total.</p>
<IndexFigure scene="writes" number={5} />
<p>PostgreSQL’s <strong>HOT</strong> update can avoid new ordinary index entries when indexed values are unchanged and the new version fits on the same heap page. Including total removes that opportunity for total changes. A non-HOT update can require entries in other ordinary indexes too, even when their own key values are unchanged. Physical storage and cleanup details remain with <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">the storage article</a>.</p>
<p>A separate pair of 10,000-row disposable tables had the same primary key, tenant/identifier index and 70% table fillfactor. Updating 1,000 totals generated 191,024 WAL bytes with the key-only index and 333,584 with total included. These are captured log bytes for one run, including its page-image conditions, not a universal multiplier, device-write count or throughput comparison. The extra indexed value changes a write obligation even though the read-side index allocations above happened to be equal.</p>
<p>Keep correctness roles visible. A primary-key or uniqueness constraint may depend on an index that also serves reads. A faster non-unique replacement does not preserve that constraint. PostgreSQL automatically creates indexes for primary-key and unique constraints, but not for the referencing side of a foreign key. An order-line prefix can help both related lookups and checks when its referenced order changes; inspect the actual definition. <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/">Expressing rules with constraints</a> owns the integrity rule itself.</p>
<p>Compare apparent duplicates by key order and direction, collation, operator class, predicate, expression, included values, uniqueness and constraint dependencies. An index on tenant alone overlaps the prefix of tenant/date, but can be smaller and cheaper for another route. Usage counters over a quiet interval do not prove an index unnecessary; rare reporting and constraint work still matter. Retire one only after checking the complete workload and dependency role.</p>

<h2 id="keep-the-candidate-only-if-its-benefit-survives-the-workload">Keep the candidate only if its benefit survives the workload</h2>
<p>For the demonstrated all-status first page, retain tenant/date/identifier as the candidate to carry into the parent’s workload comparison. It returns the same ten rows, removes the sort and reduces top-level buffer accesses from 613 to 12 in these captures. Reject status-before-date as the replacement for that all-status request: it serves a different useful range. Keep coverage and the pending partial index provisional until their query frequency and write cost earn the extra obligations.</p>
<p>The result is a justified design decision for a bounded fixture, not a deployment acceptance. Apply the completed parent’s comparison method to small and large tenants, common and rare statuses, nulls, narrow and wide date ranges, changed recent-order distributions and the application’s prepared statements. Include the related-row request, daily report and writes. Verify unchanged ordered results, then compare p95 or p99 by operation, useful throughput, failures, index size, log rate and maintenance work under representative competition. The native worksheet collected plans, results, allocations and bounded write-log evidence; it did not collect workload latency distributions or sustainable throughput.</p>
<p>State regression signals alongside the candidate: more rejected heap rows, increasing heap fetches despite coverage, a sort returning to the first-page path, growth in pending-subset size, or worse write tail latency. If the expected route is absent, investigate estimates and observed work in <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">Diagnosing a query from its execution plan</a>. Forcing index use would hide the choice you need to explain.</p>
<p>An experiment does not establish a safe live build or removal. <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">Changing schemas without breaking applications</a> owns index creation, validation and constraint replacement; <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">service maintenance</a> owns resource headroom and ongoing upkeep; <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> owns rollout and retirement gates. The design earns that operational work only when its benefit survives the workload.</p>
<p>To repeat the evidence, <a href={worksheet} download="index-workload-verify.mjs">download the native worksheet</a>. Install Node.js 22 or later and <code>pg@8.23.1</code> with <code>npm install --ignore-scripts pg@8.23.1</code> in a new directory. Set <code>PG_URL</code> to a disposable PostgreSQL administrative connection and run <code>node index-workload-verify.mjs</code>. It needs database-creation privileges, creates a uniquely named <code>rel3_a25_run*</code> database, retains <code>raw-results.json</code> beside the script and drops its database after closing its session. The fallback connection targets the local verification instance; provide your own when repeating elsewhere.</p>

